From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 22:25:15 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] drm/virtio: report the host's graphics memory pressure and
 holders

A Darwin UML host gives the guest's graphics a fixed device-memory budget
that the guest's own memory accounting cannot see, so a guest reclaimer
cannot tell when the budget is nearly spent or which process holds it.
Negotiate a private feature (VIRTIO_GPU_F_UML_MEMORY_REPORT, bit 23) with
one query command, VIRTIO_GPU_CMD_UML_GET_MEMORY, and one event,
VIRTIO_GPU_EVENT_UML_MEMORY (events_read bit 1). The reply carries the
budget, the pool's use, what the guest's contexts hold, the refusal count,
a pressure state, and up to 16 holders by process (pid, bytes, idle time,
contexts, command name), largest first.

The driver keeps the last reply and serves it from gpu_memory_report on the
primary DRM minor, a mode 0600 file for a user-space reclaimer such as
Android's lmkd. Reading formats the stored reply and never waits for the
host. Writing "arm <min_bytes> <min_idle_ms>" subscribes to the event and
sets the holder filters, "refresh" asks again and "disarm" unsubscribes; a
write only queues a work item, which keeps at most one query on the control
queue. A stored reply calls sysfs_notify(), so poll() reports POLLPRI. The
event schedules the same work item rather than config_changed_work, so a
host that holds its answers cannot delay display hotplug. The file belongs
to the minor's directory and goes with it when the DRM device is unplugged, at
shutdown as at removal; the driver stops queueing and notifying before that
and never removes the file by name. Without the feature there is no file, and
nothing changes for a guest.

The wire declarations are kernel/common/gpu/virtio_gpu_uml.h in the owning
Darwin host repository; drivers/gpu/drm/virtio/virtgpu_uml.h is a copy
that its sync script checks, together with the codes it shares with
include/uapi/linux/virtio_gpu.h.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
